# Difference Between LeetCode 112 and 437

| 112. Path Sum | 437. Path Sum III |
|---------------|-------------------|
| Return **true/false** | Return **count of paths** |
| Path must start at the **root** | Path can start **anywhere** |
| Path must end at a **leaf** | Path can end at **any node** |
| Root → Leaf only | Any downward path |
| DFS is sufficient | Prefix Sum + HashMap is the optimal solution |

---

# Key Interview Points

- Paths can start at **any node**, but they must always move **downward**.
- The brute-force approach starts a DFS from every node, resulting in **O(n²)** time in the worst case.
- The optimal solution uses **Prefix Sum + HashMap**, similar to **LeetCode 560 (Subarray Sum Equals K)**.
- Store the frequency of prefix sums and use `currentSum - targetSum` to count valid paths.
- Always **backtrack** by removing the current prefix sum before returning to the parent.
- Use **`long`** for prefix sums to avoid integer overflow.
